  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 100 AM MDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nThe upper level high will continue to be centered over the \nSouthern and Central Rockies today and Tuesday. Moisture\u00a0has \ninfiltrated the ridge at the mid and upper levels. Satellite \nimagery shows a large\u00a0area of mid and high clouds over Colorado \nand upstream across the Great Basin. This is expected to keep \nskies mostly cloudy, though a brief period of partly cloudy skies \nis possible. This will allow enough sunshine\u00a0to push highs into \nthe mid to upper 90s today. Models show the airmass is a degree or\ntwo warmer today, but the increase in cloud cover should offset \nthis. Precipitable water values will be 0.6 to 0.9 (in), which is \nslightly above normal over the higher terrain but below normal \nacross the plains. This will yield another round of high-based \nvirga showers today. DCAPE values will be 600-900 J/kg a little \nless than yesterday, which should keep wind gust speeds under 45 \nmph. Like yesterday, low level moisture may prevail over far \nnortheast Colorado where one or two strong/severe storms will be \npossible.\u00a0 On Tuesday, we'll see very similar conditions. Cloud \ncover may end up being less, and if it is, temperatures could be a\ncouple of degrees warmer. Another round of high-based virga \nshowers with gusty winds is also expected.\u00a0\n\nThe upper level high finally weakens and shifts southeast of the \nregion Wednesday. Flow aloft becomes south-southwest, transporting\nmonsoon moisture into the area. Moisture will increase slowly at \nfirst, but by Wednesday afternoon precipitable water values will \nclimb to 120 to 160 percent of normal. Scattered to numerous \nshowers and thunderstorms are expected to form over the higher \nterrain during the afternoon and then spread northeast through the\nafternoon and evening hours. Moderate to locally heavy rainfall \nwill be possible.\u00a0\n\nFor the past couple of days, models have advertised that a \nshortwave trough will track across Colorado Thursday \nafternoon/evening. Moisture continues to increase with \nprecipitable water values climbing to 140 to 180 percent of \nnormal. This should produce scattered to numerous showers and \nthunderstorms. Locally heavy rainfall could lead to isolated \nflooding.\u00a0 It will be much cooler on Thursday with highs in the \nupper 70s to lower 80s across northeast Colorado. \n\nThe upper level high is expected to stay over the southeast part \nof the country Friday and through next weekend. South-southwest \nflow is expected to persist over Colorado and monsoon moisture \nwill continue to be pulled into the state. Rounds of scattered to \nnumerous showers and thunderstorms are expected each day. \nTemperatures will remain on the cool side with highs expected to \nbe in the mid 70s to mid 80s each day.",
